1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,857
Let's talk a little bit about incremental strategies

2
00:00:02,857 --> 00:00:06,350
What you've seen so far is the so called append strategy

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:11,563
where we simply expressed our incremental predicate

4
00:00:11,563 --> 00:00:15,081
in an incremental block right in SQL

5
00:00:15,081 --> 00:00:17,783
But if you take db 2 to production

6
00:00:17,783 --> 00:00:21,026
you probably want to use a more advanced strategy

7
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:25,467
For example Snowflake and most of the data warehouse technologies

8
00:00:25,467 --> 00:00:28,330
support the so called merge operation

9
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:31,168
So you can just replace append with merge

10
00:00:31,168 --> 00:00:35,247
and this way you can have automatically duplicate detection

11
00:00:35,247 --> 00:00:38,274
and just ensure that only new records are inserted

12
00:00:38,274 --> 00:00:40,680
and all the records will be updated

13
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:43,415
For warehouses without merge support

14
00:00:43,415 --> 00:00:47,792
there is delete and insert which is like two operations

15
00:00:47,792 --> 00:00:52,960
deleting all the old records or the records that we would like to update

16
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:56,371
and then re adding everything in this incremental batch

17
00:00:56,371 --> 00:00:59,193
And for tables which are partitioned by date

18
00:00:59,193 --> 00:01:05,147
you can also update full partitions with using the insert override strategy

19
00:01:05,147 --> 00:01:08,415
I left you a link in course resources

20
00:01:08,415 --> 00:01:12,265
to see which platform supports which strategy in dbt

21
00:01:12,265 --> 00:01:16,241
Here we go you see the Snowflake supports all of them

22
00:01:16,241 --> 00:01:21,461
and I also left you an example of how to define a merge strategy

23
00:01:21,461 --> 00:01:26,784
by specifying a unique key which will be the decisive column

24
00:01:26,784 --> 00:01:32,000
on the update or insert decision as new or updated records come in

25
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,680
You see it's very much the same

26
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:37,391
we just changed the incremental strategy and added a unique key

27
00:01:37,391 --> 00:01:40,015
There is also another strategy called micro batch

28
00:01:40,015 --> 00:01:42,640
which is a very production ready strategy

29
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:45,520
is for larger tables that you want to scale

30
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:50,368
and we will cover this in detail in the production part of the course

31
00:01:50,368 --> 00:01:52,480
I'll see you in the next lecture
